          "classification": "SOURCE_FACT",
          "statement": ".b32 is a fundamental type specifier of basic type 'Bits (untyped)'; Table 8 lists .b8, .b16, .b32, .b64, .b128 under 'Bits (untyped)'.",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "PTX-9.3-MOV-023",
            "PTX-9.3-MOV-024"
          ]
        },
        {
          "semanticId": "PTX-MOV-SEM2-018",
          "classification": "SOURCE_FACT",
          "statement": "Registers (.reg state space) are fast storage locations; aside from 1-bit predicate registers, scalar registers have a width of 8-, 16-, 32-, 64-, or 128-bits (32-bit width is therefore possible for .b32 registers); registers are not fully addressable.",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "PTX-9.3-MOV-026"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "evidenceId": "PTX-9.3-MOV-023",
          "sourceKind": "HTML",
          "documentPath": "documents/sources/nvidia/ptx/9.3/raw/ptx_isa_9.3.html",
          "documentSha256": "e2095c4ae00fe81fa18325b8e26c4a41b94965295c7affd7ff7f787fd98a8856",
          "locator": {
            "section": "5.2.1",
            "heading": "Fundamental Types",
            "anchor": "fundamental-types",
            "startLine": 3019,
            "endLine": 3022,
            "page": 53
          },
          "quotation": "In PTX, the fundamental types reflect the native data types supported by the target architectures. A fundamental type specifies both a basic type and a size. Register variables are always of a fundamental type, and instructions operate on these types. The same type-size specifiers are used for both variable definitions and for typing instructions, so their names are intentionally short."
        },
        {
          "evidenceId": "PTX-9.3-MOV-023#pdf-exact-match",
          "sourceKind": "PDF",
